# Protocol — Baseline gate (Experiment 000) ({{CAMPAIGN_TITLE}})

Fires ONCE, before the loop. Experiment 000 does two jobs: it establishes the **floor** every later
method must beat, AND it validates the full {{VALIDATION_CHAIN}} chain end-to-end (so a later "win"
isn't an artifact of a broken pipeline).

## Baseline method(s)
{{BASELINE_METHODS}}
<!-- The simplest defensible method(s) — e.g. a closed-form / least-squares / matched-filter / naive
     solution. No learned or heavily-tuned components. This is the no-skill-to-low-skill reference. -->

## Trivial / degenerate references (record these too)
Also compute and record `{{PRIMARY_METRIC}}` for the **trivial/degenerate solutions** — all-zeros /
constant / mean predictor / a fully-saturated or otherwise trivial physical state — so every later
result can be checked against them. Two rules follow from these:
- A method that scores **worse than a trivial reference is not a win**, however "optimized" it looks.
- If a **non-physical** trivial answer (e.g. all-zeros) scores *better* than a plausible result, treat
  it as a **flag that the metric/units may be incongruent** (scrutinize scaling / normalization / ranges
  / the metric definition) — not as a target to beat.

